A||||5||XDrewX||carb.  vs.  fuel injection for v8 swap||||1||
Z||000000||XDrewX||10-08-2001||05:00 PM||||I need opinions on which type of v8 setup would be appropriate for my situation.<BR>Here are some factors:<P>1. This car will NOT be a daily driver.<P>2. I will be buying a new crate engine, (one that will be compatible with the fuel setup I eventually choose.)<BR>I will be putting NOS on this engine, to use for 1/4 mile runs only.  No street racing.<P>3. Archie will be putting this engine in my car.<P>4. I have played around with carburators when I was younger,forgot most of it now, but I do not know much about Fuel Injection. (I can still learn though.) <P>5. I have limited engine repair experience.<P>6. If my new V8 would "breakdown", which engine would be easier to diagnose the problem.(this is a biggie)<P>My end use of this car will be<BR>1. pleasure weekend driving, hitting a few car shows<P>2. 1/4 mile drag (once or twice a month)<P>If anyone has any 1/4 mile times for ther V8, please post them.<P>Also, does anyone have gas mileage statistics, for a V8.  I know it varies between engines and the type of setups, but any info is appreciated.<P>Thanks in advance.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by XDrewX (edited 10-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||roadwarrior||10-08-2001||11:48 PM||||I would go with a carb cause it is alot easier to dial in for weather and altitude conditions, but if you are gonna get a an aftermarket reprogramable chip for the fuel injection and the computers that go with it then go with that. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||JasonS||10-09-2001||08:46 AM||||Definately go with a carb.  Much less hassle.  I am almost finished putting a 350 in my '84.  I had too many other hassles to worry about.  I can't imagine having to figure out the wireing for fuel injection.<P>good luck,<BR>Jason||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||10-09-2001||09:14 AM||||Drew,<P>Let me start by saying I don't have a V8 Fiero... yet. But, I have owned, restored and built a variety of muscle cars. My current project is installing an 89 TPI 350 / 700r4 into a 68 Camaro. Having said that, I'll now express my opinion...<P>You did not mention a budget but you did mention that you would be buying a brand new crate motor and plan on Archie doing the install work. If that's the case, choose fuel injection. There are several sizes and performance packages available and than cane be ordered with just about everything you need to bolt it up. Reliability and economy will be improved. Trouble shooting is simplified by simply plugging in the computer and reading the trouble codes. <P>The other thing that's nice about fuel injection is that it automatically adjusts its self under different weather conditions and elevation changes. Unlike a carburetor that has to be rejetted and recurved and distributor timing that needs tweaked when ever the weather changes, the fuel injection sensors automatically adjust these to keep the engine operating at optimal levels.<P>If, however, you want to experiment with different camshafts, intakes, distributor curves, etc. a carbed motor is the way to go.<P>So to summarize... <P>Fuel injected will cost more for a new set-up but give better economy (see note below), higher reliability, hands off superior operation. <P>Carbed motor will cost less but will require constant tweaking to keep the motor running at optimal levels.<P>Note: Although a fuel injected motor will improve economy, it does so by keeping the motor running at optimal levels. However, true improvements in fuel economy are more of a result of gearing changes such as overdrive. At best expect only about 1-2 MPG diffeence between a FI motor and a properly tuned carbed motor when using the same transmission and final gear ratio. <P><BR>------------------<BR>Roy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||XDrewX||10-09-2001||04:25 PM||||As far as budget is concerned......I dont have one, my budget will be what ever it costs in the end. &lt;grin&gt;<P>Thanks for the opinions so far,<P>Bump for More !!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||10-09-2001||07:32 PM||||I had a Holley 4bbl on my 454 in the old 'Burb. I could never get it "right"- either lean at idle and rich above that or the opposite, there was no "in between". So, I tried another carb and it was worse. Then, a Holley Projection 4Di came along and the truck was never the same again! Perfect idle, awesome throttle response and economy- how about a built 454 without an overdrive in a huge truck that went from 9 MPG to 15+ mpg. Fuel injection all the way.||||reg||1
